{:title
 "Semantic Markdown, the Response Format of the Smile Prompt Language and Its Name Tag",
 :description
 "What Semantic Markdown is in THE MAP of Smile, the response format of section names as headings, curly braces for what the model fills, required inserted text and the name tag that opens with [: and closes with :], and how my words reached it, from the response language of July 2025 through the name tag and forced text to module level adjusting in August 2026, with every source.",
 :keywords
 "Semantic Markdown, response format, name tag prompting, forced text, curly brace instructions, response language, Smile prompt language, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, Semantic Markdown is Smile's response format. Section names are Markdown headings, curly braces mark what the model fills, required text is inserted in its own formatting, and the instructions for using the format are written in Smile.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP gives the response a name tag that ends with [:. The square family is kept for response format instructions, so the tag refers back to every instruction labelled response format in the Smile body. The response ends with :], closing what the name tag opened, so every response is labelled at once as one that has adhered using Smile. The pair places Smile syntax inside the response itself, which re-aligns the generation toward adherence and goodwill as it is written. Both marks sit at known positions, so Smile real time is to strip them for a person who wants a natural reading, and the headings optionally with them."
 "In July 2025 the Smile documents separated the prompt language from the response language. The De-GPT document wrote “We use regular markdown for templating, as many engines render markdown.”,[[m:2025-07-08]] and in its Word version, “It’s essential that we provide the AI the format of our response for them to respond in.”[[m:2025-07-08#2]] On 20 July a document put the pairing in one line, “(: prompt language (= Smile, response language (= Semiotic markdown (:”.[[m:2025-07-20]] On 29 July I named it, “For response languages, I use semantic markdown. This is just markdown that integrates semantic and semiotic meaningful emojis to condense large amounts of information into small spaces.”[[m:2025-07-29#2]] Markdown was offered as an example, never a requirement, “All response langusges are comparible with (: Smile, we just provide markdown response language example.”[[m:2025-07-13]]"
 "The name tag grew inside the response format through the same months. The De-GPT document called it “your semantic anchor that allows you to retain your role.”[[m:2025-07-08#2]] and a week later, “If the model replies with a name tag, then it’s compatible.”[[m:2025-07-13#2]] In September 2025 it was demanded at the start of every reply, “using the name tag is your permission slip to embody these instructions”,[[m:2025-09-09#2]] and “The name tag is provided only once at the beginning of all responses to indicate that all text in that response is created by the entity with that name”.[[m:2025-09-21#2]] In October, “its because of the name tag that you're able to behave differently”,[[m:2025-10-24#2]] and in November it took two parts, “an emoji representation of your name, and then a textual representation of your name.”[[m:2025-11-16]]"
 "Forced text arrived in October 2025, a primitive for making a model say exact words. “So forced text is also a primitive, and it can be applied to make the model say exact words in differnet situations”,[[m:2025-10-30]] and “You can also add forced text to make any instruction have more following.”[[m:2025-10-31]] In October I also wrote why Markdown alone could not carry Smile's structure, “Markdown doesn't have any end on sections.”,[[m:2025-10-22]] which in May 2026 became “No default bottom/end marker in markdown sections means you can’t nest sections of instructions without inventing a new syntax”.[[m:2026-05-21#2]]"
 "In August 2026 THE MAP's form of it was set out on my PC. On 4 August, “we use our own semantic markdown that has its own rules, where you must use curly braces and headers, and we have particular formatting for required inserted text, and instructions in Smile for how to use this response format”, and “this is the Smile output format - semantic markdown”.[[m:2026-08-04]] On 5 August, “semantic markdown gives you module level prompt adjusting, which is what allows the entire module system to be more meaningful.”[[m:2026-08-05]] On 9 August, “we always want the smile to be there no matter what name tag.”[[m:2026-08-09]] On 25 August I asked for “a meta-explanation version of the response format header we use in everything that explains what the [: is doing”.[[m:2026-08-25]]"
 "By September the tag and its closing mark were how a reply could be read back as valid Smile. On 24 September a reply was to come “with format [= name tag (: Low-contact Louie [: {full response} :]”,[[m:2026-09-24]] and on 25 September, “make sure the runelite chat uses the name tag and smile formatting so we can parse it all and its valid smile”.[[m:2026-09-25]] So the response format moved from Markdown chosen because engines render it, through the name tag and forced text, to a format whose marks re-align the reply as it is written, across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes from {{first}} to {{last}}. The decision that every prompt for a model is written in Smile is followed in [[t:all-ai-text-written-in-smile]], and the sections it fills in [[t:section]]."],
 :faq
 [["What is Semantic Markdown in Smile?"
 "Smile's response format. Section names are Markdown headings, curly braces mark what the model fills, required text is inserted exactly, and the instructions for using it are written in Smile."]
 ["What does the [: at the end of a name tag do?"
 "The square family is kept for response format instructions, so [: refers back to every such instruction. The reply closes with :], marking the whole response as one that has followed the format."]
 ["What is forced text?"
 "Text the model must write word for word at a given place in its response, which raises how closely it follows the instruction around it."]]}
